= A1
{c}
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=A1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In <atomic units>, $\hbar=m_e=e=4\pi\varepsilon_0=1$, and the <hydrogen atom> has
$$
\boxed{\hat H\Psi=-\frac{1}{2n^2}\Psi,\qquad
\hat l^2\Psi=l(l+1)\Psi,\qquad \hat l_z\Psi=m\Psi,}
$$
$$
\boxed{\hat s^2\Psi=\frac34\Psi,\qquad \hat s_z\Psi=m_s\Psi.}
$$
The allowed <quantum numbers> are
$$
n=1,2,\ldots,\qquad l=0,1,\ldots,n-1,\qquad
m=-l,-l+1,\ldots,l,\qquad m_s=\pm\frac12.
$$
Here the fixed <electron spin> quantum number is $s=1/2$. The <energy eigenvalues> are in hartrees; restoring units gives $\hbar^2l(l+1)$ and $\hbar m$ for the orbital operators and the corresponding factors for <spin>. The spin-independent hydrogen <Hamiltonian> does not split the two <spin> projections.

= b
{parent=A1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Keep one normalized <spin> function fixed and write $A=1/\sqrt{24}$ and $f_m(\theta,\phi)=\sin\theta\,e^{im\phi}$, with $m=\pm1$. Overall constants and the <spin> factor pass unchanged through the spatial operators.

First, in <atomic units>, $\hat l_z=-i\partial_\phi$, so
$$
\hat l_z\Psi=-i(im)\Psi=\boxed{m\Psi}.
$$
For the squared <orbital angular momentum>, $\partial_\phi^2f_m=-f_m$ and
$$
\partial_\theta\!\left(\sin\theta\,\partial_\theta f_m\right)
=(\cos^2\theta-\sin^2\theta)e^{im\phi}.
$$
Therefore the two terms of the angular operator give
$$
\hat l^2f_m=
\frac{-\cos^2\theta+\sin^2\theta+1}{\sin\theta}e^{im\phi}
=2\sin\theta e^{im\phi}=2f_m,
$$
using $1-\cos^2\theta=\sin^2\theta$. Thus **$\hat l^2\Psi=2\Psi$**.

Finally, the spatial hydrogen <Hamiltonian> is
$$
\hat H=-\frac12\left[\frac{1}{r^2}\partial_r(r^2\partial_r)-\frac{\hat l^2}{r^2}\right]-\frac1r.
$$
For $R=Ar e^{-r/2}$,
$$
R'=A(1-r/2)e^{-r/2},\qquad
\frac1{r^2}\frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm dr}(r^2R')
=Ae^{-r/2}\left(\frac2r-2+\frac r4\right).
$$
Substitute the already established angular <eigenvalue> $l(l+1)=2$:
$$
\hat H(RY\sigma)=AY\sigma e^{-r/2}
\left[-\frac12\left(\frac2r-2+\frac r4\right)+\frac1r-1\right]
=-\frac18RY\sigma.
$$
Hence **$E=-1/8$ hartree**, consistently with $n=2$. The angular calculation must precede the radial substitution if one wants to avoid differentiating the entire product repeatedly.

= c
{parent=A1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Denote the two complex spatial <atomic orbitals> by $\psi_+$ and $\psi_-$, retaining the same <spin> state. With the phases specified by the supplied <spherical harmonics>, choose
$$
\boxed{\psi_x=\frac{\psi_- -\psi_+}{\sqrt2},\qquad
\psi_y=\frac{i(\psi_-+\psi_+)}{\sqrt2}.}
$$
The two original <atomic orbitals> are <orthonormal>, so the squared coefficients add to one and these combinations are normalized and mutually <orthogonal>. Explicitly,
$$
\psi_x=\frac{1}{\sqrt{32\pi}}\,x\,e^{-r/2},\qquad
\psi_y=\frac{1}{\sqrt{32\pi}}\,y\,e^{-r/2},
$$
which are real spatial <wavefunctions>. An overall sign on either orbital has no physical consequence.

These are the familiar **$2p_x$ and $2p_y$ <p orbitals>**. Each has two lobes of opposite <wavefunction> sign, with an <atomic-orbital nodal plane> through the <atomic nucleus>: $x=0$ for $2p_x$ and $y=0$ for $2p_y$. The sign is a phase convention, not a positive or negative <electric charge>; the <probability density> is nonnegative on both lobes.

\Image[paper-1-real-2p.png]
{title=Original sections through the real hydrogen 2p <atomic orbitals>, showing opposite phases and their nodal planes}

= d
{parent=A1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Linearity and the shared $l=1$ angular <eigenvalue> give
$$
\boxed{\hat l^2\psi_x=2\psi_x,\qquad\hat l^2\psi_y=2\psi_y.}
$$
Using the relative phases in part (c),
$$
\boxed{\hat l_z\psi_x=i\psi_y,\qquad\hat l_z\psi_y=-i\psi_x.}
$$
Thus each real orbital remains an <energy eigenstate> with **$E=-1/8$ hartree** and has definite <orbital angular momentum> magnitude **$\sqrt2\hbar$**, but neither is an <eigenfunction> of $\hat l_z$. A measurement of $l_z$ gives **$+\hbar$ or $-\hbar$, each with <probability> $1/2$**. Its mean is zero and its variance is $\hbar^2$; a zero mean does not mean that a measurement gives zero.

= A2
{c}
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=A2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $\{\phi_j\}$ be a complete <orthonormal eigenbasis> of the <Hamiltonian>, with $H\phi_j=E_j\phi_j$ and $E_j\geq E_0$. Expand a nonzero admissible <trial wavefunction> as $\widetilde\Psi=\sum_j c_j\phi_j$. Then
$$
\langle\widetilde\Psi|\widetilde\Psi\rangle=\sum_j|c_j|^2,
\qquad
\langle\widetilde\Psi|H|\widetilde\Psi\rangle=\sum_j|c_j|^2E_j.
$$
Consequently
$$
\widetilde E-E_0
=\frac{\sum_j|c_j|^2(E_j-E_0)}{\sum_j|c_j|^2}\geq0,
\qquad\boxed{\widetilde E\geq E_0.}
$$
Every numerator term is nonnegative and the denominator is positive. Equality requires the trial to lie wholly in the <ground-state subspace>; it need not be an individually chosen eigenvector when the ground <energy> is degenerate. This is the <quantum variational principle>, and <wavefunction normalization> before calculation is optional because the quotient removes the overall scale.

= b
{parent=A2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

<Wavefunction normalization> requires $\alpha>0$. For the normalized <probability> weight proportional to $e^{-\alpha x^2}$, the <Gaussian integral> and its derivatives give
$$
\langle x^2\rangle=\frac1{2\alpha},\qquad
\langle x^4\rangle=\frac3{4\alpha^2}.
$$
Since $\widetilde\Psi''=(\alpha^2x^2-\alpha)\widetilde\Psi$, the <kinetic energy> and <potential energy> expectations are
$$
\langle T\rangle=\frac\alpha2-\frac{\alpha^2}{2}\langle x^2\rangle=\frac\alpha4,
\qquad
\langle V\rangle=\frac12\langle x^4\rangle=\frac3{8\alpha^2}.
$$
Thus
$$
E(\alpha)=\frac\alpha4+\frac3{8\alpha^2},\qquad
E'(\alpha)=\frac14-\frac3{4\alpha^3}=0
\ \Longrightarrow\ \alpha_*=3^{1/3}.
$$
The second derivative is positive, and the <energy> diverges at both ends of $\alpha>0$, so this is the global minimum within the <Gaussian function> family. **The best <Gaussian function> upper bound is**
$$
\boxed{E_0\leq E(\alpha_*)=\frac{3^{4/3}}8\simeq0.54084359.}
$$
This is an upper bound, not the exact quartic ground <energy>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**A gives the same optimum:** replacing $\alpha$ by $2\alpha$ merely reparameterizes the same set of positive-width <Gaussian functions>, so its best bound is unchanged.

**B is worse as a ground-energy estimate:** it has odd parity and a node at the origin, whereas the <ground state> of this even confining one-dimensional potential is even and nodeless. It is therefore <orthogonal> to the exact <ground state> and belongs to the odd sector, whose lowest <energy> is already above the ground <energy>; the extra factor $x$ cannot be removed by varying its width. A separate numerical minimization gives a best value about $1.92372$ for B, well above the original <Gaussian function> bound.

**C can improve the bound:** setting $\beta=0$ recovers every original <Gaussian function>, while nonzero $\beta$ adds shape freedom. Minimizing over this larger family can never give a worse optimum, and the additional freedom does lower the quartic-oscillator estimate. Inclusion of a family alone proves “no worse”; improvement is not guaranteed for every conceivable potential, for example one already solved exactly by a <Gaussian function>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For any $\alpha>0$, the <Gaussian function> is the exact nodeless ground <wavefunction> of a <quantum harmonic oscillator> with
$$
\boxed{\hat H_{\rm ho}(\alpha)=-\frac12\frac{\mathrm d^2}{\mathrm dx^2}+\frac12\alpha^2x^2,\qquad E_{0,\rm ho}=\frac\alpha2.}
$$
Direct substitution cancels the $x^2$ terms. An additive constant in the potential would simply shift the <eigenvalue>.

A natural comparison uses the same optimized width as part (i), $\alpha=3^{1/3}$. The harmonic ground <energy> is then **$0.72112479$**, compared with the quartic <Gaussian function> expectation **$0.54084359$**. For this common normalized trial,
$$
\langle V_{\rm quartic}\rangle=\frac\alpha8,
\qquad \langle V_{\rm ho}\rangle=\frac\alpha4,
$$
while the <kinetic energy> <expectation value> is $\alpha/4$ in both cases. The quartic potential lies below this harmonic potential for $0<|x|<\alpha$ and above it for $|x|>\alpha$; the difference weighted by the <Gaussian function> is negative, giving the lower expectation.

If the standard unit-frequency oscillator is chosen instead, $\alpha=1$ gives $E_{0,\rm ho}=1/2$, below the optimized quartic upper bound. These are different harmonic comparison potentials, not contradictory answers. Crossing potential curves cannot be ordered globally; the comparison must specify the frequency or <Gaussian function> width used.

\Image[paper-1-variational-potentials.png]
{title=Original comparison of the quartic potential with harmonic potentials at unit frequency and at the optimized Gaussian width}

