= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The gross requirement is **an anisotropic <polarizability tensor>**, so that changing molecular orientation changes the induced response. A permanent <electric dipole moment> is not required; a homonuclear diatomic can therefore have a rotational <Raman scattering> spectrum.

For the inelastic lines of a linear <rigid rotor>, the detailed rule is **$\Delta J=\pm2$**, with $J\geq0$. The $\Delta J=0$ contribution is unshifted <Rayleigh scattering>, not a rotational <Stokes Raman scattering> line. <Stokes Raman scattering> has $\Delta J=+2$ because the molecule gains rotational <energy>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a transition starting at $J$ and ending at $J+2$, the molecule gains
$$
\Delta\widetilde\varepsilon
=\widetilde B\big[(J+2)(J+3)-J(J+1)\big]
=\widetilde B(4J+6).
$$
<Energy> conservation therefore gives the scattered-light <wavenumbers>
$$
\boxed{\widetilde\nu_{\rm S}(J)=\widetilde\nu_L-(4J+6)\widetilde B,\qquad J=0,1,2,\ldots.}
$$
The first shifts below the laser are **$6\widetilde B,10\widetilde B,14\widetilde B,18\widetilde B$**. Adjacent Stokes lines are separated by **$4\widetilde B$**, while the first line is $6\widetilde B$ from the Rayleigh line. The figure uses positive <Raman shift> $\widetilde\nu_L-\widetilde\nu_{\rm S}$; increasing shift means decreasing scattered-light wavenumber.

\Image[paper-1-rotational-raman.png]
{title=Original line-position sketches for the diatomic and BF3 rotational Raman series; stick heights carry no intensity information}

= b
